        </nav>

        <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-6 py-16 md:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
            <aside class="hidden lg:block">
                <TableOfContents headings={toc} class="sticky top-28" />
            </aside>

            <article class="win-guide min-w-0">
                <section id="what-to-watch" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>01</span> · What to watch</p>
                    <h2>Counters to watch on a Windows server</h2>

                    <div class="guide-answer">
                        <p><strong>Windows server monitoring</strong> means checking a server's CPU, memory, disks, network, services and event logs on a schedule, keeping the history, and getting an alert when something stays outside its normal range.</p>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        Windows measures all of it already. <strong>Performance counters</strong> are the numbers Windows keeps for every part of the system, named like <code>\Memory\Available MBytes</code>: an object, then a counter. Services report their state to the <strong>Service Control Manager</strong>, and everything else that happens lands in the <strong>event log</strong>. Every monitoring tool reads these sources; tools differ in how they collect, store and alert on them.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A slow or failing server shows it in a handful of signals. Treat the thresholds as starting points: a database server that runs at 90% CPU every night is normal for that server.
                    </p>

                    <div class="resource-grid">
                        {resources.map((r) => (
                            <div class="resource-card" style={`--type: ${r.color}`}>
                                <span class="type-badge">{r.name}</span>
                                <dl>
                                    {r.signals.map((s) => (
                                        <div>
                                            <dt><code>{s.path}</code></dt>
                                            <dd>{s.watch}</dd>
                                        </div>
                                    ))}
                                </dl>
                            </div>
                        ))}
                    </div>
                    <p class="guide-caption">Each card's color marks the same resource in the tables, the Collector diagram and the code on this page.</p>

                    <h3 id="commit-charge" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${color.memory}`}></span>Commit charge, not only free RAM</h3>
                    <p>
                        Windows backs every committed allocation with RAM or the page file. The most it can commit is the <strong>commit limit</strong>: physical memory plus the current page file size. When the commit charge reaches the limit and the page file cannot grow, allocations fail. Processes crash with out-of-memory errors and the System log records event 2004, even while <code>Available MBytes</code> still shows free RAM. <code>% Committed Bytes In Use</code> is the charge as a share of the limit.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="disk-latency" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${color.disk}`}></span>Disk latency, not disk busy time</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>% Disk Time</code> can read above 100% on RAID arrays and SANs and says little about whether requests are slow. <code>Avg. Disk sec/Read</code> and <code>Avg. Disk sec/Write</code> measure how long each request took, queueing included. Local SSDs answer in a few milliseconds. Latency above about 20 ms with a queue that never drains points at storage.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="event-ids" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${color.events}`}></span>Event IDs worth an alert</h3>
                    <p>
                        Counters do not tell you that a server rebooted or a service crashed. These events do:
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Log</th><th>Event ID</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Resource</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {eventIds.map((e) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>{e.log}</td>
                                        <td><code>{e.id}</code></td>
                                        <td>{e.meaning}</td>
                                        <td><span class="type-badge" style={`--type: ${color[e.resource]}`}>{resourceName[e.resource]}</span></td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="built-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>02</span> · Built in</p>
                    <h2>Built-in Windows monitoring tools</h2>
                    <p>
                        Windows Server ships with enough to diagnose a problem on one machine. Each tool reads the same counters at a different depth:
                    </p>

                    <LanguageTabs tabs={builtInTools.map(({ id, label }) => ({ id, label }))} label="Built-in tool" group="maple-guide-windows-tool">
                        {builtInTools.map((t) => (
                            <LanguageTab id={t.id}>
                                <div class="tool-panel">
                                    <p class="tool-panel__open"><span>Open</span><code>{t.open}</code></p>
                                    <p set:html={t.body} />
                                    {t.id === "perfmon" && <pre><code set:html={code(logmanExample, "powershell")} /></pre>}
                                    {t.id === "powershell" && <pre><code set:html={code(getCounterExample, "powershell")} /></pre>}
                                    <p class="tool-panel__limit">{t.limit}</p>
                                </div>
                            </LanguageTab>
                        ))}
                    </LanguageTabs>

                    <p>
                        <strong>Event Viewer</strong> (<code>eventvwr.msc</code>) reads the System, Application and Security logs and saves filters on level, source and event ID as custom views. <strong>Windows Admin Center</strong> is a free browser-based console from Microsoft that connects to many servers and shows each one's performance charts, events, services and processes.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">The gap they leave</p>
                        <p>
                            None of the built-in tools sends you a notification when a counter on one of twenty servers crosses a line, and only data collector sets keep history. Every other type of tool on this page adds those two things.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="tool-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>03</span> · Tool types</p>
                    <h2>Types of Windows server monitoring tools</h2>
                    <p>
                        Windows server monitoring software falls into the groups below. They read the same counters and logs; they differ in how the data is collected, where it is stored, who maintains the templates and how it is priced. Examples are listed alphabetically.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Fits</th><th>Trade-offs</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {toolTypes.map((row) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td class="guide-table__key">{row.type}</td>
                                        <td>{row.examples}</td>
                                        <td>{row.fits}</td>
                                        <td>{row.tradeoffs}</td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="open-source" class="scroll-mt-24">Open-source Windows server monitoring tools</h3>
                    <p>
                        The open-source agents differ mostly in the format they send. <a href="https://github.com/prometheus-community/windows_exporter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">windows_exporter</a> serves metrics in the Prometheus format on port 9182 for a Prometheus server to scrape. <a href="https://github.com/influxdata/telegraf/tree/master/plugins/inputs/win_perf_counters" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Telegraf's win_perf_counters input</a> reads any counter for InfluxDB and other outputs. The <a href="https://www.zabbix.com/documentation/current/en/manual/concepts/agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zabbix agent</a> and <a href="https://nsclient.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NSClient++</a> report to Zabbix and Nagios-compatible servers.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The <strong>OpenTelemetry Collector</strong> covers counters, service states and event logs in one agent and sends them over OTLP, the OpenTelemetry protocol most observability backends accept. The rest of this guide uses it.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">Checklist for choosing a tool</h3>
                    <div class="guide-checklist">
                        {choosing.map(([title, body], index) => (
                            <div><span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span><div><h4>{title}</h4><p>{body}</p></div></div>
                        ))}
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="collector" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>04</span> · Setup</p>
                    <h2>Windows Server monitoring with OpenTelemetry</h2>
                    <p>
                        The OpenTelemetry Collector runs on the server as a Windows service. Its Windows receivers read those sources, and one exporter sends the results to a backend over HTTPS:
                    </p>

                    <figure class="flow" aria-label="Data flow. On the Windows server, system APIs and performance counters feed the hostmetrics and windows_perf_counters receivers, the Service Control Manager feeds windows_service, and the event log feeds windows_event_log. The Collector sends hostmetrics data to the Maple Hosts page, counters and service states to the Metrics page, and events to the Logs page.">
                        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 240" aria-hidden="true">
                            <defs>
                                <marker id="flow-arrow" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
                                    <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z" class="flow__head" />
                                </marker>
                            </defs>
                            <rect x="0" y="30" width="190" height="196" rx="8" class="flow__box" />
                            <text x="16" y="54" class="flow__title">Windows Server</text>
                            <rect x="12" y="68" width="166" height="68" rx="6" class="flow__pill" />
                            <text x="95" y="98" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__label">System APIs and</text>
                            <text x="95" y="114" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__label">performance counters</text>
                            <rect x="12" y="144" width="166" height="30" rx="6" class="flow__pill" />
                            <text x="95" y="163" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__label">Service Control Manager</text>
                            <rect x="12" y="182" width="166" height="30" rx="6" class="flow__pill" />
                            <text x="95" y="201" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__label">Event log</text>

                            <rect x="250" y="30" width="270" height="196" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--collector" />
                            <text x="266" y="54" class="flow__title">otelcol-contrib service</text>
                            {receivers.map((r, i) => {
                                const y = 68 + i * 38;
                                return (
                                    <g>
                                        <rect x="262" y={y} width="246" height="30" rx="6" class="flow__pill" />
                                        <text x="274" y={y + 19} class="flow__mono">{r.name}</text>
                                        {r.covers.map((id, j) => (
                                            <circle cx={494 - (r.covers.length - 1 - j) * 12} cy={y + 15} r="4" style={`fill: ${color[id]}`} />
                                        ))}
                                    </g>
                                );
                            })}

                            <line x1="178" y1="102" x2="260" y2="83" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line" />
                            <line x1="178" y1="102" x2="260" y2="121" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line" />
                            <line x1="178" y1="159" x2="260" y2="159" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line" />
                            <line x1="178" y1="197" x2="260" y2="197" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line" />

                            <text x="549" y="73" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--accent">OTLP</text>
                            <rect x="580" y="30" width="180" height="196" rx="8" class="flow__box" />
                            <text x="596" y="54" class="flow__title">Maple</text>
                            <rect x="592" y="68" width="156" height="30" rx="6" class="flow__pill" />
                            <text x="670" y="87" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__label">Hosts</text>
                            <rect x="592" y="106" width="156" height="68" rx="6" class="flow__pill" />
                            <text x="670" y="144" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__label">Metrics</text>
                            <rect x="592" y="182" width="156" height="30" rx="6" class="flow__pill" />
                            <text x="670" y="201" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__label">Logs</text>

                            <line x1="508" y1="83" x2="590" y2="83" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line flow__line--out" />
                            <line x1="508" y1="121" x2="590" y2="121" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line flow__line--out" />
                            <line x1="508" y1="159" x2="590" y2="159" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line flow__line--out" />
                            <line x1="508" y1="197" x2="590" y2="197" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow)" class="flow__line flow__line--out" />
                        </svg>
                    </figure>
                    <p class="guide-caption">The dots on each receiver are the resources it covers. Every metric, including the hostmetrics ones behind the Hosts page, can also be charted on the Metrics page.</p>

                    <div class="receiver-list">
                        {receivers.map((r) => (
                            <div>
                                <p class="receiver-list__name"><code>{r.name}</code>{r.covers.map((id) => <span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${color[id]}`} title={resourceName[id]}></span>)}</p>
                                <p set:html={r.body} />
                            </div>
                        ))}
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        Older Collector releases use earlier names for these receivers: <code>windowseventlog</code> before v0.150, <code>windowsservice</code> before v0.154 and <code>windowsperfcounters</code> before v0.160. v0.163 still accepts the old names as deprecated aliases.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">1. Install the Collector as a Windows service</h3>
                    <p>
                        The <a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-releases/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">opentelemetry-collector-releases</a> project publishes an MSI for the Contrib distribution, the build that includes the Windows receivers. It installs to <code>C:\Program Files\OpenTelemetry Collector</code> and registers an automatic-start service named <code>otelcol-contrib</code> that runs as LocalSystem, an account that can read the Security log. From an elevated PowerShell prompt:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(installExample, "powershell")} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="config" class="scroll-mt-24">2. Write the config</h3>
                    <p>
                        Replace <code>config.yaml</code> in the install folder with this file. It sends everything to Maple; for another backend, change the exporter. Keys are colored by the resource they collect.
                    </p>
                    <div class="code-file">
                        <p class="code-file__name">C:\Program Files\OpenTelemetry Collector\config.yaml</p>
                        <pre><code set:html={code(collectorConfig, "yaml")} /></pre>
                    </div>

                    <ul class="guide-notes">
                        <li>The three <code>*.utilization</code> metrics are off by default in <code>hostmetrics</code>. Maple's Hosts page reads them, and they give percentages rather than raw byte counts.</li>
                        <li><code>resourcedetection</code> adds <code>host.name</code>, <code>os.type</code> and <code>host.arch</code> to every metric and log, so data from many servers stays separable.</li>
                        <li>A counter that does not exist on a server logs a warning at startup and the Collector keeps running. On IIS servers, add the <code>iis</code> receiver to the metrics pipeline for connection and request counts.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3 id="ingest-key" class="scroll-mt-24">3. Give the service its ingest key and restart</h3>
                    <p>
                        The config reads <code>MAPLE_INGEST_KEY</code> from the environment. Set it on the service's registry key: a new machine-wide variable is not passed to services until the Service Control Manager restarts, which in practice means a reboot. Then validate the file and restart:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(serviceEnvExample, "powershell")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        If the service stops again right away, the reason is in <strong>Event Viewer → Windows Logs → Application</strong> under the source <code>otelcol-contrib</code>.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="event-log-collection" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="type-dot" style={`--type: ${color.events}`}></span>Event log collection</h3>
                    <p>
                        The System and Application logs are small enough to collect in full. The Security log on a domain controller or a busy file server can write thousands of events a minute, so the config selects only failed logons, lockouts and audit log clears with an XML query. Add the event IDs your security team reviews to its <code>Select</code> element.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Each event arrives with the channel, provider, event ID, level, rendered message and its own data fields. Critical becomes <code>FATAL</code>, Error <code>ERROR</code>, Warning <code>WARN</code> and Information <code>INFO</code>, so a backend can count errors by severity without parsing the message. <code>file_storage</code> keeps each receiver's position on disk, so a restart resumes where it stopped, and <code>exclude_providers</code> drops the Collector's own messages from the Application log.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="maple" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>05</span> · In Maple</p>
                    <h2>Windows servers in Maple</h2>
                    <p>
                        With the config pointed at Maple, each kind of data lands on its own page.
                    </p>

                    <ol class="guide-steps">
                        <li>
                            <span>01</span>
                            <div><h4>Find the server under Infrastructure → Hosts</h4><p>Every machine that sends <code>hostmetrics</code> data is listed by <code>host.name</code>, with CPU, memory and disk usage, 15-minute load and last-seen time. A host's page charts CPU, memory, filesystem per mountpoint, network per device and load.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/windows-server-monitoring-01-hosts.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Hosts page row for the Windows server WIN-IIS01: Windows, amd64, CPU, memory and disk usage bars, 15-minute load and last-seen time." width="2340" height="324" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>02</span>
                            <div><h4>Chart counters and service states on the Metrics page</h4><p>Search for <code>windows.</code> to find the counter metrics and <code>windows.service.status</code>. Open one, choose an aggregation, filter by an attribute such as <code>attr.direction = "read"</code>, group by <code>attr.name</code> or <code>attr.instance</code>, and add it to a dashboard.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/windows-server-monitoring-02-service-status.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Metrics page for windows.service.status with the min aggregation, grouped by attr.name: W3SVC and WinRM flat at 4 (Running) over the last hour." width="2340" height="1340" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>03</span>
                            <div><h4>Read events on the Logs page</h4><p>Filter <strong>Service</strong> to <code>windows-eventlog</code>, then by <strong>Severity</strong>. Search for an event ID or a provider name as text. A record's detail sheet shows the host it came from.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/windows-server-monitoring-03-event-logs.webp" data-zoomable alt="The detail sheet of an ERROR record from the windows-eventlog service: System channel event 7031, the World Wide Web Publishing Service terminating unexpectedly, with host.name WIN-IIS01 under Resource attributes." width="1344" height="2036" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>04</span>
                            <div><h4>Alert on high CPU</h4><p>Open <code>system.cpu.utilization</code>, set the aggregation to <code>avg</code> and the filter to <code>attr.state = "idle"</code>, then click <strong>Create alert</strong>. Fire when the value is below <code>0.15</code> over a 10-minute window: less than 15% idle is more than 85% busy. Metric rules count data points as samples, and this filter gives one per CPU core every 30 seconds, so a 2-core server sends 40 in 10 minutes. Lower <strong>Min samples</strong> from its default of 50 to match your smallest server.</p></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>05</span>
                            <div><h4>Alert on a stopped service</h4><p>Open <code>windows.service.status</code> with <code>attr.name = "W3SVC"</code> and the aggregation <code>min</code>, so one stopped copy on any server pulls the value down. Create an alert that fires below <code>4</code>, with <strong>Min samples</strong> at 1. If the Collector itself stops, the rule sees no data; turn on <strong>Alert when there is no data</strong> to catch that too.</p></div>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3 id="per-server-alerts" class="scroll-mt-24">One alert per server</h3>
                    <p>
                        A metric query rule can group by <code>service.name</code> or a data point attribute, but <code>host.name</code> is a resource attribute, so the CPU rule above evaluates the average of every server that sends the metric. For one incident per server, write the rule as <strong>Raw SQL</strong> and return a <code>group</code> column:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(perHostCpuSql, "sql")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Set the condition to below <code>0.15</code> and <strong>Reduce buckets by</strong> to <strong>Average</strong>. The <code>samples</code> column is what <strong>Min samples</strong> sums; without it, every row counts as one sample.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">A silent server does not fire a grouped rule</p>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Alert when there is no data</strong> is not available on grouped rules, and a Raw SQL rule with a <code>group</code> column only counts as empty when no server returns rows at all. A server whose Collector stops drops out of the results: no new incident opens for it, and an open one stays open until its data returns. Watch for it on the Hosts page, where a host with no report for 5 minutes shows <strong>Ended</strong>.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Set it up in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Send a Windows server to the Hosts page</h3>
                        <p>The Hosts docs have the minimal <code>hostmetrics</code> config, the metrics each column reads, the status rules and troubleshooting for hosts that do not appear.</p>
                        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                            <a href="/docs/infrastructure/hosts" class="guide-button guide-button--primary">Set up host monitoring</a>
                            <a href="/docs/alerting/alert-rules" class="guide-button">Alert rules</a>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="guide-sources">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">Sources and further reading</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/tree/main/receiver/hostmetricsreceiver" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector Contrib: Host Metrics Receiver</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/tree/main/receiver/windowsperfcountersreceiver" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector Contrib: Windows Performance Counters Receiver</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/tree/main/receiver/windowseventlogreceiver" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector Contrib: Windows Event Log Receiver</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/tree/main/receiver/windowsservicereceiver" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector Contrib: Windows Service Receiver</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/tree/main/receiver/iisreceiver" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry Collector Contrib: IIS Receiver</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-counter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft Learn: Get-Counter</a></li>
